Using WinSCP with the Raspberry Pi!

Step 1: Download WinSCP
-	https://winscp.net/eng/download.php

Step 2: Open WinSCP
 
Note: Your Laptop and Raspberry Pi need to be connected to the same WIFI for this to work. PuTTY is the same way as most SSH terminals. 
-	It’s just like using PuTTY, you want to type in your Raspberry Pi’s IP address in Host name, then you want to type in your Username on the Pi.
-	Don’t worry about typing in the password on the password line; it will have you type it in again anyway. 
-	Then use ‘SFTP’ under ‘File Protocol’, and make sure the port number is ‘22’, after this just hit Login.
 
Also, the great thing about WinSCP is that it remembers where you left off, so if you close out of it while inside a specific folder, it will open inside that folder you were in. 
For example, I was in the folder ‘python_scripts’ after I exited out of the app, then once I logged in again, I was back inside that folder, right where I left off!

Step 3: Give yourself ‘Read and Write’ privileges on the Raspberry Pi
-	sudo chmod 777 /target/directory
o	Please use the 777-permission code with caution, as this gives everyone on the Raspberry Pi read/write/execute privileges. We use it because, as an outside user, we need to be able to send files over to the Raspberry Pi.

Step 4: Begin transferring files from your Laptop/Desktop to the Raspberry Pi
-	This is very simple, just hover over the file you want, hold down the left keypad button, and drag it over to where you want on the Raspberry Pi.
Note Important: If you are unable to move a file over to a folder on the Raspberry Pi, this means you need ‘write’ privileges. So, it is important to know which command is what.

Number Symbolic Meaning: r = read, w = write, x = execute
Number	Symbolic	Meaning
777	rwxrwxrwx	Everyone: read/write/execute
755	rwxr-xr-x	Owner: all, Group & Others: read/execute
700	rwx------	Owner: all, Group/Others: none
644	rw-r—r--	 Owner: read/write, Groups/Others: read
600	rw-------	Owner: read/write only

Quick Reference:
Code	Owner	Group	Others	Typical User
777	rwx	rwx	rwx	Open access (not secure)
755	rwx	r-x	r-x	Programs/scripts
700	rwx	---	---	Private scripts/data
644	rw-	r--	r--	Regular files (docs, config)
600	rw-	---	---	Private files

